/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Start with the recorded appearance, then add context.
   - No single element decides a class.
   - An interpretation key is a written or pictorial table that links a land cover to its expected appearance: tone, shape, texture, season and usual neighbours.
   - Aerial photographs come from aircraft: large scale, high detail, flown on demand, but limited repeat coverage.
   - Season changes the target.
   - Multi-date visual comparison places two or more images of the same area side by side.
   - Scale relates a distance on the image to the same distance on the ground.
   - S is the scale ratio; dp is the photo distance; Dg is the ground distance in the same unit; M is the denominator of the representative fraction.
   - At 1:50,000, one centimetre on the image represents 50,000 cm on the ground, which is 500 m.
   - S is the scale of a vertical photo; f is the camera focal length; H is the flying height above datum; h is the ground height above datum; f and H − h in the same unit.
*/
