/* book exam traps: High overall accuracy does not remove class confusion. A class can be right 95 percent of the time where the map shows it (user's accuracy) and still miss many of its real pixels (low producer's accuracy). Read both before trusting a class. | Radiometric correction is about brightness, geometric correction about position. Marks are lost by swapping the two. | A contrast stretch changes only the display values, not the stored reflectance. Do not claim it corrects the image. | NDVI is (NIR − red) ÷ (NIR + red). Reversing the order gives a negative index and a wrong map, and water genuinely has a negative NDVI. | User's accuracy divides by the row (mapped) total and producer's accuracy by the column (reference) total. Swapping them reverses the meaning: in the worked example built-up is 0.810 one way and 0.750 the other. */
